/** \page bumr_dialog Configuring backup and mirroring

This dialog displays how backups and mirroring are configured. This
configuration will be set up when the user first runs ZZALOG, 
but this dialog allows the user to check that the configuration is correct 
and change it if necessary.

\image html bumr_dialog_1.png "Tab showing backup and mirroring configuration"
\image latex bumr_dialog_1.png "Tab showing backup and mirroring configuration"

\section bumr_mirror_summary Mirror summary

The top of the dialog shows a summary of the mirroring currently in place.

The first line summarises the mirroring configuration. It will say one of the following:
- <B>Unknown</B> The mirroring configuration cannot be determined. This should
only happen before the user has set up mirroring as part of a cold-start.
- <B>Mirror not used - target file only</B> Mirroring is not being used. 
The log is being written to the target file.
- <B>Mirror being used - working on target file</B> Mirroring is being used and the
log is being written to the target file. The mirror file will be 
updated when ZZALOG is closed.
- <B>Mirror being used - working on mirror file</B> Mirroring is being used
and the log is being written to the mirror file. The target file will be
updated when ZZALOG is closed.
- <B>Mirror being used - target file not available</B> Mirroring is being used
but the target file is not available. The log is being written to the mirror file. 
The target file will be updated when ZZALOG is next opened when the target file
is accessible.

This configuration allows the user to specify whether to use mirroring and if so,
where the mirror file is located, and whether the mirror file or the target file 
is used as the working file. The working file is the file that is currently being written to.
Unless accessing the target file impacts the performance of ZZALOG, 
the target file should be used as the working file.

\section bumr_backup Backup

A backup us a copy of the log file that is taken when ZZALOG is started.
This is a safety measure to ensure that if the log file is corrupted, 
the user can recover most of their data from the backup. 
The backup should be stored in a different location to the log file.

The number of backups to keep is also specified in the <B>Depth</B> field. 
If this number is zero, then no backups will be kept. 
If this number is greater than zero, then the specified number of backups will be kept. 
The backup files will be named <i>[logfile].adi.[N]</i> where [logfile] is the name of the log file and 
[N] is a number from 1 to the specified depth.
The latest backup will be named <i>[logfile].adi.1</i> and the earlier
backups will be renumbered upto the depth. The eldest backup will
be deleted.

This dialog allows the user to specify the location of the backup files 
and the number of backups to keep.

*/